WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.980
Database errors shown to users should be safe and limited.

2
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:13.997
They should not expose SQL text, table names, column names, connection strings, stack traces, internal paths, tokens, or sensitive record details.

3
00:00:13.997 --> 00:00:22.299
A user usually needs to know whether input should be corrected, a request can be retried, or support should be contacted.

4
00:00:22.299 --> 00:00:25.524
They do not need internal database diagnostics.

5
00:00:25.524 --> 00:00:31.425
Authorized operators need useful detail, but logs should still avoid sensitive values.

6
00:00:31.425 --> 00:00:42.334
Structured logging can include operation names, sanitized validation outcomes, dependency names, timing, categories, correlation identifiers, and safe context.

7
00:00:42.334 --> 00:00:52.078
It should avoid raw secrets, full connection strings, sensitive record contents, and unnecessary query text that would create disclosure risk.

8
00:00:52.078 --> 00:00:56.057
Correlation IDs or request identifiers help investigation.

9
00:00:56.057 --> 00:01:09.711
A suspicious validation failure, authorization denial, repeated database error, or administrative data operation may involve an API request, service-layer code, a database call, and a background job.

10
00:01:09.711 --> 00:01:16.161
Correlation lets operators connect events without placing sensitive data into every log entry.

11
00:01:16.161 --> 00:01:32.354
Security-relevant signals include validation failures around query options, authorization failures on data operations, repeated database errors, unusual query patterns, export activity, reporting access, and administrative data changes.

12
00:01:32.354 --> 00:01:39.352
These signals can help teams detect problems, tune controls, and identify areas that need remediation.

13
00:01:39.352 --> 00:01:44.498
Detection supports prevention; it does not replace safe query construction.

14
00:01:44.498 --> 00:01:50.673
Logs can reveal that something needs attention, but they cannot make an unsafe query safe.

15
00:01:50.673 --> 00:02:03.779
The right design prevents variable data from becoming command structure, limits database permissions, returns safe errors, and preserves enough operational context to investigate responsibly.
